#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The README screenshots, from a demo with made-up data: docs/img/dashboard.png, session-browser.png and
dashboard-narrow.png.

Everything happens in a work folder (a new temp folder, or --work DIR):
  - a fake HOME holding invented Claude sessions (transcripts, handoff notes, rate limits, running processes), SLURM
    jobs and a Spotify track. Only the theme files (~/.tmux/theme.conf, ~/.local/share/dash-theme, ~/.config/btop)
    are copied in from the real home, so the colours match. Nothing in the real ~/.claude or ~/.config is written;
  - a private tmux server, `tmux -L dashdemo`, whose panes all see that HOME. The default tmux server is never touched;
  - stubs for `ssh` (canned squeue / sacct: nothing reaches the cluster) and `osascript` (a fixed track: the real
    Spotify is never asked or controlled). btop shows cpu and memory only: its process list would show real command
    lines, its net box the machine's IP address. The machine's load, its sensors and the weather (Tokyo, Open-Meteo)
    are live.
A tmux client is attached in a pseudo-terminal, its output fed to pyte (a terminal emulator, installed into a venv in
the work folder when it is missing), and the screen drawn as HTML, then as PNG by headless Chrome. A frame that shows
your user name or home folder is not written.

usage: demo/make-screenshots [--work DIR] [--out DIR] [--keep]   (--keep: leave the demo server running to look at it,
       `tmux -L dashdemo attach`, then `tmux -L dashdemo kill-server`)
"""
